<p>The Trump administration is discussing with the Democratic Republic of Congo whether up to 1,100 Afghans who assisted U.S. forces and were evacuated to Qatar could be resettled there, according to reporting first disclosed by The New York Times and confirmed by Reuters-based coverage and other outlets. The group has been stranded in Qatar while awaiting U.S. visas, and the talks come after President Trump moved to shut down a pathway that had allowed them to apply for resettlement in the United States, while visa processing for Afghan nationals was reported to be effectively halted. The proposal would leave many of those Afghans facing a stark choice: relocation to Congo or return to Taliban-ruled Afghanistan. More than four years after the U.S. withdrawal from Kabul, the case highlights the legal and policy barriers that still stand in the way. It has drawn attention because it involves people who worked alongside U.S. forces and remain in limbo despite being evacuated from Afghanistan, underscoring both the unresolved legacy of the war and the urgency of finding a safe, durable outcome.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/3J3Pi5pApplN1RNH8vIwBht8hP83FaSxXJfc8RLi5jbwokI3GDz2GU5uqdsYJUSBXWid02yrkXKf932L5jiAhhVVJ-Ig_JjYo051vrZKigBcGzmqgsJ2YGsKxJmGu8Jw-6Nd6170RR3w54v7jOBpUEdkFs2coBvMU2Zdvg_caeqVPtJDjKiU049D4aG3sCjVPIPthluWTnInNmJexIW7SUz3RKsjxw' alt='A photograph released by the U.S. Army showing Afghans being processed at Camp As Sayliyah, in Qatar, in August 2021.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Qatar limbo: The Afghans at the center of the talks were evacuated to Qatar and have remained there while waiting for U.S. immigrant visas that advocates say are no longer moving forward.</li><li>Advocacy role: A nonprofit representing the group confirmed the Congo discussions, offering outside verification of a plan U.S. officials had not publicly announced.</li><li>Choice described: Coverage in Deutsche Welle and AllAfrica described the decision facing these Afghan allies as a choice between resettlement in the DRC and life under Taliban rule in Afghanistan.</li><li>Withdrawal legacy: Reuters-based reporting noted that the people involved remain in legal limbo more than four years after the U.S. withdrawal from Kabul in 2021.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Advocacy organization: The group representing the Afghans said talks are underway about resettling roughly 1,100 people in the Democratic Republic of Congo after they were left stranded in Qatar. (<a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/04/us-talks-resettle-1100-afghans-congo-group-says'>Al-Monitor</a>)</li><li>The New York Times: Its reporting said the evacuees would face a choice between moving to Congo and living under the Taliban, underscoring the stakes for Afghans who had aided U.S. forces. (<a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/04/21/world/europe/afghan-refugees-congo-us.html'>The New York Times</a>)</li><li>Deutsche Welle: DW described the proposal as forcing Afghan allies into a stark resettlement decision shaped by insecurity in both their past and possible future destinations. (<a href='https://www.dw.com/en/us-in-talks-to-send-afghan-refugees-to-dr-congo/a-76889628'>Deutsche Welle</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/apr/21/afghan-refugees-us-congo'>Trump officials consider sending 1,100 Afghans who aided US forces to Congo</a> - theguardian.com</li><li><a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/04/us-talks-resettle-1100-afghans-congo-group-says'>US in talks to resettle 1,100 Afghans in Congo, group says</a> - al-monitor.com</li><li><a href='https://allafrica.com/stories/202604220092.html'>Congo-Kinshasa: U.S. In Talks to Send Afghan Refugees to DR Congo</a> - allafrica.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dw.com/en/us-in-talks-to-send-afghan-refugees-to-dr-congo/a-76889628'>US in talks to send Afghan refugees to DR Congo</a> - dw.com</li><li><a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/04/21/world/europe/afghan-refugees-congo-us.html'>Trump Is Said to Be in Talks to Send Afghans Who Aided U.S. Forces to Congo</a> - nytimes.com</li></ul>